WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.133
How to actually start.

2
00:00:02.433 --> 00:00:06.700
Enough to be genuinely literate, without eating your agent and eval time.

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:08.967
Here is the plan.

4
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:13.712
Weekend one: simulate qubits, gates, and a Bell state in raw NumPy.

5
00:00:13.712 --> 00:00:16.233
It is linear algebra you already know.

6
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:22.296
Weekend two: use Qiskit or PennyLane, and run the Bell circuit on free real hardware.

7
00:00:22.296 --> 00:00:26.567
Seeing noise in your own results teaches more than any diagram.

8
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:31.600
Weekend three: train a variational classifier on a toy two feature dataset.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:33.767
Which toolkit?

10
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:37.967
Qiskit has the biggest ecosystem and the best documentation.

11
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:43.800
PennyLane is built for quantum machine learning, with autodiff that plugs into PyTorch and JAX.

12
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.200
Pick Qiskit for circuits, PennyLane for machine learning.

13
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.700
You do not need a dilution refrigerator.

14
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.967
IBM Quantum has a free tier.

15
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:55.500
Amazon Braket lets you queue circuits.

16
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.300
And so does Azure Quantum.

17
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.410
Where does this touch real work?

18
00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.067
Two places.

19
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:05.227
First, a paper on quantum optimization for satellite scheduling.

20
00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:08.967
You can tell leadership whether the speedup survives scrutiny.

21
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:14.033
Second, and far more likely, a security review asks if you are post quantum ready.

22
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:18.267
You will know what ML-KEM is, and what a migration plan looks like.

23
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:23.200
A trap in weekend three: stopping once the quantum model works.

24
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:30.100
Train an sklearn SVM on the same data, compare, and understand why the classical one wins.

25
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.237
Let's recap.

26
00:01:32.237 --> 00:01:34.267
Weekend one, NumPy qubits.

27
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:37.333
Weekend two, an SDK on real hardware.

28
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:41.200
Weekend three, a variational circuit against an SVM.

29
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.704
The likeliest payoff is post quantum readiness.

30
00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:47.567
Next up: compute frontiers beyond the GPU.
